comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-18T14:08:40.386Z",codex-ecosystem-planner,"Immutable-proof review 2026-07-18: the live tracker has 30,491 activity entries across 1,874 streams; every one of the 1,866 current items has at least one recorded event, and validation reports zero missing streams, hash mismatches, chain mismatches, or drifted items. Mutation-op inventory includes create/update/claim/release/close, annotations, linked artifacts, test runs, plans, compact/repair/redact, and even edit/delete operations as recorded events. Remaining guarantee extensions are already individually tracked: pm-klo8 for workspace singleton state, pm-9yfo for contract-enumerated mutation completeness, pm-4e12 for atomic multi-entity commits, pm-g5sx for branch-safe merge/reconciliation, pm-3z0k for portable attestations, and pm-u14c for principal signatures. Point-in-time non-mutating read pm-hib1 and restore/recovery foundations are shipped. No new history item needed."
  "2026-07-21T15:56:04.897Z",codex-tracker-governance,"Immutable-proof review 2026-07-21: the pre-mutation tracker exposed 31,782 activity entries across 1,909 streams; every one of the 1,901 current item ids appeared in activity, every sampled event carried before and after hashes, and workspace validation reported zero history drift. Eight history-only ids correspond to recorded delete operations rather than current items. Existing completion work remains canonical for workspace-scoped mutation coverage, contract-enumerated mutation completeness, atomic commits, branch reconciliation, portable attestations, signatures, and restore. Historical metadata debt was not rewritten with placeholders."
notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-25T06:23:33.859Z","harness:claude-code","Immutable-history assessment 2026-07-25, written against the standing requirement that every mutating action be permanently recorded, that the record be unalterable after the fact, and that any item be restorable to any prior version.\n\nRecorded: every mutating CLI and SDK action appends to a per-item append-only JSONL stream; workspace-scoped mutations (schema, config, profile, init) have their own hash-chained audit stream; the stream is content-addressed and chained, so an edit to any entry breaks verification downstream; history --verify --strict-exit makes that check a gate rather than a report; redaction, repair, and compaction all exist as governed, recorded operations rather than as file edits. Restoration exists in both directions: point-in-time read projection (get --at) reconstructs historical state without mutating, and restore replays a stream to recover a deleted or missing item, with hash validation.\n\nTwo properties are asserted rather than proven, and both are filed. Completeness - that no mutating path can skip the append - is asserted by convention today; pm-9yfo makes it a contract-driven test enumerated from the action table, which is the only form that survives new commands being added. Independent verifiability - that a third party can confirm the record without trusting this tooling - needs the detached attestation bundle in pm-3z0k, complemented by per-entry author signatures in pm-u14c so that the answer to who recorded this is as strong as the answer to whether it was altered.\n\nOne honest limitation to record rather than paper over: immutability here is tamper-evident, not tamper-proof. A party with write access to the repository can rewrite a stream; what they cannot do is rewrite it without the chain revealing the rewrite, provided a verifier holds an earlier anchor. That is why the attestation bundle matters more than any additional in-repository control - it moves the anchor outside the trust boundary. The acknowledgment flow shipped this pass is the correct shape for the same reason: unknown-author events are dispositioned by appending an audited acknowledgment, never by attributing authorship retroactively.\n\nThe remaining practical risk is growth rather than integrity: 33.5k history entries across 1,983 streams today, with compaction available but its cadence unautomated. That is pm-o79o, and it becomes load-bearing at the decade-shaped workspace the outcome milestone targets."
body: "## Charter\nLiving capability epic for storage, append-only history, and data durability: TOON item files, per-item history streams (hash-chained JSONL), checkpoints, gc, compaction, export/import, and format_version gating.\n\n## Scope\n- In: item-store write paths, history chain integrity (repair/redact/compact), gc scopes (locks/checkpoints/embeddings), backup/restore, format migrations.\n- Out: concurrency/locking semantics (pm-dj98 owns races and atomicity), search index storage (pm-ydp6).\n\n## Invariants this epic guards\n- History is append-only and hash-chained; appendLineAtomic is the only writer (see pm-y2ml docs).\n- Items must survive strict→lenient TOON decode fallback (2026-05-24 recovery precedent).\n- pm health history_drift must stay at 0 across 1400+ items.\n\n## Status\n20 closed children (compact snapshot, history-compact bulk + policy, gc scopes, restore hardening). Open as a living backlog — no open children at 2026-07-04; new durability work lands here.\n\n## Update 2026-07-06\nNew initiative epic parented here: **pm-9rxu — Scale-out** (sub-second, bounded-token context at 100k-1M items, thousands of concurrent agents). Owns workspace-size physics: read index, directory fan-out, pagination, claim throughput, history/git growth policy. ADR: pm-bl8x (under pm-m2u1). Boundary: pm-dj98 keeps correctness races; pm-9rxu owns throughput/scale.\n\n## Update 2026-07-07 — immutable-proof invariant hardening\nFull all-status backlog review found the append-only-proof guarantee is complete for items (restore-to-version pm-9lc, file recovery pm-g6qd, per-stream --verify, workspace drift scan) but not for workspace-level state. Two new children:\n- **pm-klo8** — workspace-scoped audit history stream: schema/config/profile/init/extension-state mutations get the same append-only hash-chained JSONL treatment as items (coordinate with pm-bgcu locking; feeds pm-e200).\n- **pm-9yfo** — history completeness gate: contract-enumerated test proving every mutating CLI/SDK action appends a history entry (phase 1 item-scope now; phase 2 workspace-scope after pm-klo8; shrinking-only allowlist ratchet)."
